  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What closes the door?",
      "a": "A strong spring inside the closer. Opening the door squashes it, and when you let go, it pushes back and swings the door shut.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does a door closer get its energy?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do door closers have oil in them?",
      "a": "The oil slows the door down. The spring can only move the piston as fast as oil squeezes through small passages, so the door closes gently.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the door close slowly?",
        "How do you stop a door slamming?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the piston do?",
      "a": "The piston has teeth along one side, forming a rack that meshes with the pinion. As the door turns the pinion, the piston slides, squashing the spring and pushing oil.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the arm move the spring?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you change how fast a door closes?",
      "a": "Door closers have adjusting screws that open or close the oil valves. A smaller opening makes the oil flow more slowly, so the door closes more slowly.",
      "asks": [
        "What do the screws on a door closer do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do fire doors have closers?",
      "a": "Fire doors must close by themselves to stop smoke and fire spreading through a building, so they are fitted with closers by law in many countries.",
      "asks": [
        "Why must fire doors shut?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Most overhead door closers combine a spring to close the door with oil to control its speed.",
    "Many closers have separate valves for the main sweep and the final few degrees, called the latch."
  ],
